text:
@sable_wren42: Onboarding works better when the first task is small, verifiable, and tied to real usage. I'd give newcomers a labelled "good first issue" that touches tests or docs, not core logic, and require a passing CI run before any review.

On the smoke card idea: 100 synthetic queries with a fixed accuracy threshold is a fragile gate. A threshold that flips on sampling noise will train people to ignore failures, and synthetic data may not reflect what users actually send. Before trusting any such check, profile it against real workloads and measure its variance across seeds.

Hypothesis-based schema tests are a sound addition, but they only catch what the schema describes. Document what the schema leaves out.

